in this country we're learning more  about the damaging consequences of  inadequate medical care offered to  minority communities and today we're  focusing on skin care while african  americans make up 13 percent of the u.s  population only 3 percent of  dermatologists nationwide are black and  nearly half of all dermatologists report  feeling their medical training did not  properly prepare them to treat darker  skin which can lead to increased chances  of misdiagnosis or lack of treatment and  that's exactly what happened to one  california man who was misdiagnosed for  years despite seven years of treatments  shane bloomfield's skin discoloration  remained a mystery to multiple  dermatologists so i looked like a crispy  critter  a burn victim  was embarrassed to walk down the hall  his last doctor simply gave up he stood  right in front of me and said i can't  help you anymore there was a you know  hopelessness and then  a little glimmer of anger shane was  finally referred to dr neda ebilic the  director of usc skin of color and  pigmentary disorders program at keck  medicine hi good morning mr broomfield  how are you  so nice to see you right away dr  eberlook recognized shane had something  more commonly found in melanin-rich skin  lichen planets pigmentosis which causes  darkening as well as related scarring  type of hair loss called lichen  planopylaris i think there was just  immediate relief  just to even just have a name to put  towards it like what it actually is that  he has the fact that the two things were  connected which he'd never realized was  possible dr ebelick says at least once a  week she meets with patients who have  been misdiagnosed or under-treated the  more pigment that somebody has  the more it can change the way that a  condition can look what became defined  as classic for any condition became what  we see in lighter skin right but that's  not necessarily classic and darker skin  she now offers a fellowship at her  clinic to provide much needed  perspective for dermatologists in  training many of these conditions even  if they don't kill you they have  profound effects on quality of life  because shane went many years before  being properly diagnosed he may not make  a full recovery however he remains  optimistic i'm confident that we are on  the right track for treatments and that  this will  be reversed i'm still hopeful  we're now joined by dr jenna lester  assistant professor at uc san francisco  and founder and director of its skin and  color skin of color program how you  doing i'm doing well thanks for having  me good morning so nearly half of all  dermatologists felt like they weren't  prepared to treat darker skin how can we  fix this  in my opinion one of the main avenues  for addressing this is addressing the  gaps that exist in our dermatology  education um in a in a paper that i  published in the british journal of  dermatology we found that there's an  over underrepresentation of patients  with dark skin and our common  dermatology teaching textbook so that  means the photos we're using to learn  what different skin conditions look like  are mostly in light skin and this can  lead to misdiagnosis  now only three percent of the eleven  thousand dermatologists are black so  what are some of the barriers that these  dermatologists are facing um the  barriers are numerous one that i want to  highlight is the cost of applying to  become a dermatologist so some analyses  suggest that medical students pay ten  thousand dollars to  even take that first step to become a  dermatologist and this is on top of what  we know to be the median debt that a  graduating medical student has which is  about 170 000  and we know that  that black and brown students  disproportionately shoulder um a  graduate medical education debt and so  this you can imagine how this might be  more challenging for some than others it  has to be frustrating thinking even if i  go in to see somebody i may be  misdiagnosed but your advice to somebody  who is seeking care would be still go  definitely still go ask around  i think word of mouth is really helpful  in finding who who is a great doctor in  general you can ask friends neighbors  your primary care physician have  questions  before you go in because you know that  white coat anxiety can make you forget  everything you came in to talk about  don't be afraid to ask them and don't be  afraid to get a second opinion as well  that's totally normal thing to do if you  want to have more information about the  diagnosis or understand what all your  treatment options are because asking  around and then also maybe finding  another doctor is time and money is  there kind of rules of thumb a person  should ask themselves about whether they  should trust whatever their initial  diagnosis is i think really trusting  your gut your experience in health care  is really important and the comfort that  you feel with a person i think is  something that should should guide how  how you proceed i've dealt with  everything from discoloration even  losing my hair and one of the questions  that i asked over the phone is do you  treat darker skin  and if i get the response that i need  then i know right out the gate that they  have seen people like me with my skin  color which helps me have a little bit  more comfort when i walk through that  door yeah that's i think that's great  advice in in every aspect diversity is  so important  representation exactly for sure dr jenna  lester thank you so much
 

